# An sview element has no value equality, so stage0 refuses to build the hash set.
# The DECLARATION alone is the trigger — the opposite of the dict rule, where a bare
# `d: mutable dict[sview, i64] = {}` is accepted and only indexing it is refused.
def f() -> void:
    s: mutable set[sview] = {}
    _ = s
# (No .neg counterpart: EVERY valid set program declines in the driver's BARE mode — set
# lowers to std definitions — so a valid-set fixture would add a permanent reject-gap.
# membership_set_rhs.neg covers the "valid element types are not flagged" side instead.)
